CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is part of a worked example asking to evaluate the line integral of (xy + z^3) ds from (1, 0, 0) to (-1, 0, pi) along a helix. The figure shows a 3D rectangular bounding box with x ranging from -1 to 1, y from -1 to 1, and z from 0 to pi. A single helical curve C spirals inside the box from the labeled starting point (1, 0, 0) at the bottom to the endpoint (-1, 0, pi) at the top, with a directional arrow indicating the orientation of traversal. The figure shows the geometry of the helix C in xyz-space, establishing the path and its orientation for setting up the arc-length line integral using the parametric equations x = cos t, y = sin t, z = t for t in [0, pi].

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Rectangular bounding box (wireframe, black thin lines) spanning x ∈ [-1,1], y ∈ [-1,1], z ∈ [0, π]
  - Interior back-corner edge lines of the box (the three edges meeting at the hidden interior corner, drawn as visible thin lines as in the source)
  - Helical curve C: x = cos t, y = sin t, z = t for t ∈ [0, π], drawn as a thick light-blue tube/line
  - Directional arrowhead on the helix roughly two-thirds along (near t ≈ 2), pointing upward along the direction of increasing t
  - Filled blue dot marking start point (1, 0, 0) on the lower-left box edge
  - Filled blue dot marking end point (-1, 0, π) on the top edge
  - Text label '(1, 0, 0)' with a small leader arrow below-left of the start dot
  - Text label '(-1, 0, π)' above-left of the end dot
  - Axis letter labels 'x' (bottom front), 'y' (upper right), 'z' (left vertical)
  - Tick value labels: x: '1' at front-bottom corner, '0' at mid bottom edge, '-1' at right-bottom corner; y: '0' at top back corner, '1' at upper-right corner; z: '0' at lower-left, 'π' at upper-left
  - Moving sample point marker on the helix (added, for the t slider)
  - Short highlighted arc-length segment ds around the sample point (added)
  - Readout panel showing t, (x, y, z), integrand xy + z³, and ds (added)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World units = math units. Box: x from -1 to 1 (2 units), y from -1 to 1 (2 units), z from 0 to π ≈ 3.1416 (3.14 units), so the box is noticeably taller than wide, matching the source. Orientation as read from the image: +z is screen-up (left vertical edge carries labels 0 at bottom, π at top); +y increases toward screen-right (label '0' at the far/top back corner near the endpoint, '1' at the upper-right corner); +x increases toward the viewer/front-left-bottom ('1' at the near bottom corner, '0' at the middle of the bottom front edge, '-1' at the lower-right corner). Camera therefore looks from roughly the +x, -y-ish octant slightly above eye level, with the three interior edges meeting at the hidden back corner drawn visible. Helix: r(t) = (cos t, sin t, t), t ∈ [0, π]; |r'(t)| = √2 so ds = √2 dt, total length √2·π ≈ 4.44. Integrand f = xy + z³ = cos t sin t + t³; the worked integral ∫_C (xy + z³) ds = √2 ∫_0^π (cos t sin t + t³) dt = √2 π⁴/4. Start dot at (1,0,0) lies on the front-left bottom edge; end dot at (-1,0,π) on the top rear edge. Colors: curve, dots and arrowhead light blue #29ABE2 (line width ~4px or tube radius 0.03); box wireframe black #000000 thin (~1.5px); labels black serif italic for x, y, z and upright for numerals; background white.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitView",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The curve is a genuine 3D helix winding around the z-axis inside the box, not a flat arc — rotating reveals that its projection onto the xy-plane is the upper unit semicircle."
  },
  {
    "id": "tParam",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Parameter t",
    "range": [
      0,
      3.14159,
      0.01
    ],
    "default": 0,
    "teaches": "How the parametrization x = cos t, y = sin t, z = t sweeps the point from (1,0,0) to (-1,0,π): position, integrand value xy + z³, and the arc-length element ds = √2 dt all change together as t advances."
  },
  {
    "id": "displayMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Show",
    "options": [
      "Curve only",
      "ds segment",
      "Integrand f = xy + z³"
    ],
    "default": "Curve only",
    "teaches": "That the line integral is built by weighting each small arc-length piece ds by the scalar field value at that point — the highlighted ds segment and the integrand height make the sum concrete."
  },
  {
    "id": "traceCurve",
    "type": "animation",
    "label": "Trace C from (1,0,0) to (-1,0,π)",
    "loop": false,
    "teaches": "The orientation of C: traversal runs from the bottom point upward along the helix, matching the arrowhead in the figure."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Start of the path",
    "narration": "At t = 0 the point sits at (1, 0, 0) on the bottom of the box, the labeled starting point of C.",
    "state": {
      "tParam": 0,
      "displayMode": "Curve only"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "A quarter turn up",
    "narration": "At t = π/2 the point is at (0, 1, π/2): the helix has swung a quarter turn in y while rising halfway up the box.",
    "state": {
      "tParam": 1.5708,
      "displayMode": "ds segment"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Weighting by the integrand",
    "narration": "Each arc piece ds = √2 dt is multiplied by f = xy + z³ = cos t sin t + t³; watch the weight grow rapidly as z climbs.",
    "state": {
      "tParam": 2.4,
      "displayMode": "Integrand f = xy + z³"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "End of the path",
    "narration": "At t = π the point reaches (-1, 0, π) at the top back corner, completing the half-turn helix.",
    "state": {
      "tParam": 3.14159,
      "displayMode": "Integrand f = xy + z³"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.65
}

NOTES: Render the box as 12 LineSegments plus the three interior corner edges (no faces, or very faint translucent faces so the helix stays visible from all orbit angles). Build the helix with a TubeGeometry over a CatmullRom/parametric curve sampled at ~200 points for smoothness; add a ConeGeometry arrowhead tangent to the curve at t ≈ 2.0. Labels ('(1, 0, 0)', '(-1, 0, π)', axis letters, tick numbers) as CSS2DObject/sprite HTML so they stay screen-facing during orbit; the small leader arrow to (1,0,0) can be a thin line. For 'ds segment' mode, highlight the sub-arc from t to t+0.15 in a saturated orange (#F5821F) tube of slightly larger radius. For 'Integrand f' mode, draw a vertical stem from the sample point whose length is proportional to f(t)/f(π) (clamped to ~1.5 units) plus a numeric readout. Keep OrbitControls damped, target at (0, 0, π/2), initial camera distance ~9 units.

Follow the interaction plan above.

Here is the generated code to evaluate:

<!-- @FIGURE_UI_BEGIN -->
<div style="font-weight:600;font-size:12px;line-height:1.35">
  Helix <i>C</i>: <b>r</b>(<i>t</i>) = (cos&nbsp;<i>t</i>, sin&nbsp;<i>t</i>, <i>t</i>),&nbsp; 0 &le; <i>t</i> &le; &pi;
</div>
<label>Parameter <i>t</i> = <span id="tVal">0.00</span>
